A movement system needs nerves and blood supply (@msk-supply: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @musculoskeletal-system, #source @med-source-osu, #source @med-source-upper-limb-nerves, #medicine @msk-healing, #orthopedics @msk-healing) { r_0B7GSTVKPXTQPSTZVF7SPYGPK1
**Read a general account, then a region:** complete the peripheral neural anatomy in [Anatomy & Physiology — Oregon State University](../sources/Anatomy-and-Physiology.lingua#r_1WEZWZ5YY6797K4EXVYTBJS25N), chapter 13, and the relevant vessel structure and exchange sections in chapter 20. Then read the upper-limb overview and brachial-plexus relationships in [Upper-limb nerves — NCBI Bookshelf](../sources/Upper-limb-nerves.lingua#r_6G2RDX948Y8KGMDFRV226X4RH6). Detailed named shoulder branches are assigned later after their muscles are familiar.

A muscle's name, attachment, action, nerve supply, and blood supply answer different questions. Sensory pathways also provide information from skin and deeper tissues. Joint function depends on this living supply and control, not only on a list of muscle actions.

A spinal root and a named peripheral nerve occupy different positions in the network. An abnormal sensation in a region cannot identify the precise site of a problem by itself. Weakness can reflect pain-limited effort, muscle or tendon injury, altered activation, or neurological disease, among other explanations.

Arteries, veins, lymphatics, and nerves travel through regions where injury or compression can affect more than one structure. Descriptions of a neurovascular examination concern this combined assessment.

**Try:** create a table for one muscle with separate columns for attachment, action, innervation, and arterial supply. Leave a cell blank until a source supports it. Explain why a muscle diagram and a nerve-distribution diagram cannot replace one another.

